A||||6||NASAReeves||HELP!! (I dropped it...)||||9||
Z||000000||NASAReeves||05-14-2001||06:43 PM||||I was taking my upper plenum off, when one of the loosened hose clamps (from the PCV elbow that joins the plenum on the underside) fell off.  I could hear it when it came off, so I carefully started looking everywhere for it.  I didn't see it, so I went ahead & started stuffing rags in all of the "ports" if you will.  Well, I found out where my hose clamp went...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">.  Out of the six exposed "ports," it went in the one that is the the top right hand corner.  <P>I'm thinking of going to buy a small, but strong, magnet to see if I can get it out that way.  If all else fails, I might have to take that part of the manifold apart, too (I don't know if I can do that), or have it towed.<P>Suggestions//Comments (other than how stupid that was of me)//HELP!!!<P>------------------<BR>Scott & Traci<BR>'88 GT||||reg||9
Z||000001||NASAReeves||05-14-2001||07:21 PM||||Whew!! I remembered years ago that my aunt/uncle gave me one of those 3' long flexible sticks with a magnet on the end of it.  I've had it for years, and never used it...until today.  After a few tries, I managed to get the crimp in the open long enough for my wife to ring it with a screwdriver.  <P>Lessons learned:  ALWAYS stuff the ports up when you have the plenum off!<P>------------------<BR>Scott & Traci<BR>'88 GT||||reg||14
Z||000002||Wade87GT||05-14-2001||07:35 PM||||Glad to hear your story turned out alright. A friend of mine was putting a larger carb on his newly rebuilt truck motor when he lost a carb stud. He finally found the stud when he cranked the engine, which then broke a piston and scored the cylinder wall pretty bad. Of course, he had to yank the motor and pay for a partial rebuild.||||reg||14
Z||000003||spideyfoot||05-14-2001||09:30 PM||||You could try my new invention. Vacuum cleaner hose to aquarium syphon pipe and tube (w cheescloth in between) then for tiny areas duc tape aquarium airline hose to the end||||reg||1
Z||000004||Stan86SE||05-14-2001||10:40 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by NASAReeves:<BR><B>Lessons learned:  ALWAYS stuff the ports up when you have the plenum off!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Amen to that!!  Whew!  Glad to hear that you were able to retrieve it!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Also, congrats on deciding to do the plenum removal and vac line/EGR tube installations on your own!  I know you've got it "whipped" now!<P>Good luck!<P>- Stan<P><p>[This message has been edited by Stan86SE (edited 05-14-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000005||StuGood||05-15-2001||11:12 AM||||Step 1.  Fish out with magnet-on-a-spring tool.  Be glad hose clamps are steel, and manifold castings are aluminum  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> !<P>Step 2.  If Step 1 didn't get it, just pull the fuel rail and intermediate manifold.<P>...I think that's what I would do, FWIW.||||reg||3
Z||000006||mrfixit58||05-15-2001||11:19 AM||||The vacuum cleaner is a good idea. In the past, I've used my chop vac with small diameter vacuum hose taped to the vacuum cleaner hose. It's real flexible and does a good job of holding onto things until you can snatch it.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||1
